===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|yue|鬼佬|tr=gwai<sup>2</sup>-lou<sup>2</sup>}}, from {{m|yue|鬼|tr=gwai<sup>2</sup>|t=[[messy, confusion, disorder, or dirt]][[unfamiliar, foreign, alien]][[ghost]]}} + {{m|yue|佬|tr=lou<sup>2</sup>|t=[[guy]], [[man, mid-age guy]], }}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|s|gweilo}}

# {{lb|en|colloquial|ethnic slur}} A [[white]] [[person]] in [[China]], {{lb|en|particularly}} a man; a ‘[[foreign]] [[devil]]’.
#* '''1977''', {{w|John Le Carré}}, ''The Honourable Schoolboy'', Folio Society 2010, p. 179:
#*: She had ruffled the bed and laid a frilly nightdress on the floor because so far as the block was concerned Phoebe was the half-'''''kwailo''''' bastard who whored with the fat foreign devil.

====Usage notes====
Some expatriates in [[Hong Kong]] now use '''gweilo''' to jokingly refer to themselves.

